deliveries of groceries and other  products have never been more important  or more in demand but that is putting a  major strain on companies that provide  the service this week Don Dahler visited  a fulfillment center for online  wholesaler boxed to see how they keep  the packages moving inside this massive  warehouse 30 minutes outside of New York  City every day essentials that have been  so hard to come by  sit in stacks as far as the eye can see  employees on forklifts do their best to  get them out the door and into delivery  trucks but even for a wholesaler used to  selling in bulk it's anything but  business as usual chiquan the co-founder  and CEO of boxed says there's a kink in  the supply chain this doesn't much look  like a shortage to me yeah so the supply  chain and kind of how boats are  supplying us is really what needs to be  tweaked we're just basically putting it  down putting on the ground because it's  going out so quickly at this moment  boxed has been described as the Costco  for Millennials it offers big boxes of  items food and household goods without a  subscription for sale solely through its  app and website in a world where people  are being asked to stay home and when  goods are in scarce supply wholesalers  like box have become lifelines to  millions of Americans Fong says boxed  customers typically receive shipments in  two days or less  but an extreme spike in demand about  three weeks ago has put them in a hole  there just now digging out up we found  some items such as toilet paper are  still moving extremely slowly from  warehouse to house that two-week stock  up it's really starting to affect how  fast we move stuff why is it so hard to  get your hands on enough toilet paper to  satisfy the demand it's been even  challenging for us to keep all those  items in stock and so you know between  toilet paper hand sanitizer and wipes I  get text messages from friends and  family that I haven't talked to in years  that suddenly text me and I feel like  I've become the most popular person in  my high school about 20 years too late  you know just like I haven't talked to  you in like 20 years but you have toilet  paper the supply chain of America is  great robust but something that's so  great and so robust can't kind of turn  on a dime they could only fill up and  put so many trucks on the road right  exactly for companies like boxed it's  the best of times and the worst of times  an enormous increase in customers and  brand recognition during a global deadly  crisis Marshall Cowan is retail analyst  for NPD consumers are going to get used  to and actually like some of the  components that come out of necessity  for shopping in this you know homebound  environment the ability to be able to  service the guests during times of real  challenged environment that really  creates a long list of loyalty no one  especially not shaquan could have  predicted this moment he grew up just 20  miles down the road in Edison New Jersey  he started boxed seven years ago  shipping inventory out of his parents  two-car garage the privately held  company now has annual revenues of a  hundred million dollars the  quintessential American success story  and from just very humble beginnings in  a garage to now a fully automated  facility with professionals all around  the country joining us it's been quite a  interesting and humbling ride in  addition to this 250 thousand  square-foot warehouse in Union New  Jersey boxed also has two other  fulfillment centers one in Dallas and  one in Vegas in order to keep up with  demand they've added new staff members  and a third shift in order to keep  workers more separated part of the  increased demand is coming from  municipalities on the day we visited  officials from Hong's hometown Edison  picked up provisions for its emergency  management employees we have people up  in the office now trying to procure a  few different vendors and stuff and a  lot of them they just they can't